  "slug": "min-cost-climbing-stairs",
  "title": "Min Cost Climbing Stairs",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Dp 1d",
  "patterns": [
    "dynamic programming",
    "fibonacci"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "define reach(i) as the cheapest cost to arrive standing on stair i - what are reach(0) and reach(1)?",
    "to arrive at stair i you came from either stair i-1 or stair i-2, paying that stair's cost - which recurrence does that give you?",
    "the answer isn't reach at the last stair - it's reach at the position one and two stairs past the last one, whichever is cheaper",
    "you only need the previous two reach values at any point, so roll them forward instead of storing a full array"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "confusing 'the cost to reach stair i' with 'the cost to leave stair i', which flips the recurrence",
    "forgetting the top is one step past the last index, and stopping the recurrence at the last cost value",
    "assuming you must start at stair 0, when starting at stair 1 for free is also allowed"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a list of `cost`, where `cost[i]` is the price you pay to\nstep on stair `i`. From stair `i` you may move to stair `i+1` or stair\n`i+2`, and you may begin your climb standing on stair 0 or stair 1 for\nfree, finishing once you've stepped past the last stair.\n\n**Return the minimum total cost to finish the climb.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "cost.length >= 2",
      "you may start on stair 0 or stair 1 for free",
      "the top is one step past the last stair (index cost.length)"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "all costs are 0 (answer is 0)",
      "cost.length == 2 (only two choices for where to start)",
      "costs alternate cheap and expensive, forcing a zig-zag choice"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "cost = [10, 15, 20]",
        "output": "15",
        "expectedOutput": "15",
        "explanation": "start at stair 1 (free), pay 15, then jump two stairs past the top"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "cost = [1, 100, 1, 1, 1, 100, 1, 1, 100, 1]",
        "output": "6",
        "expectedOutput": "6",
        "explanation": "start at stair 0 and always take the cheap steps: 1+1+1+1+1+1 = 6"
      }
    ],
